/**
  @page examples-density_optimization-index Density optimization examples
  @brief Follow a material field through a state solve, sensitivity and density update.

  Density optimization varies a material field on a fixed domain. The field
  determines the coefficients of a state equation, while an objective assigns
  a cost to the resulting state and material usage.

  @tableofcontents

  @section examples-density-start Prerequisites

  The @ref examples-pdes-poisson "Poisson tutorial" introduces the scalar
  diffusion solve and field export. The
  @ref guides-variational "variational formulations guide" explains how
  coefficients enter an assembled form.

  @section examples-density-workflow State, sensitivity and update

  The thermal example proceeds through four operations:

  1. Evaluate conductivity from the current density and solve for temperature.
  2. Solve the adjoint equation and assemble the objective derivative.
  3. Regularize that derivative to obtain a spatial update field.
  4. Update the density, project its nodal values into the admissible interval,
     and record the objective and material usage.

  @section examples-density-tutorial Thermal design

  - @subpage examples-density_optimization-simp_temperature_minimization —
    SIMP conductivity, an adjoint sensitivity and a projected density update
    on the unit square.

  The tutorial includes the computed density and temperature, the measured
  objective history, output fields and a link to the complete program.
  Its fixed-step iteration budget defines the recorded experiment. A
  stationarity test or a prescribed material budget requires a corresponding
  optimization criterion.

  @section examples-density-next Continue

  The @ref examples-shapeoptimization-index "shape optimization examples"
  vary an explicit material boundary. Both representations require a state
  equation, a consistent derivative and an update policy.

  @m_footernavigation
*/
